INT. SPEAKEASY BEHIND ABERNETHY & SONS FUNERAL PARLOR - NIGHT (1926)

A low cellar dressed as a mourning room. Black crepe on the walls. Coffins serve as tables, lids propped for drinks. A gramophone scratches out a muffled foxtrot. Cigar smoke curls under a tin ceiling.

MADAME CELESTINE DUBOIS, 50s, proprietress, in black beads and a veiled hat, sits behind the bar polishing a glass. Eyes like flint.

OTIS "SHORTY" WASHINGTON, 30s, bartender, small, quick, in a white apron stained gray. He pours without looking.

At a coffin table, HORATIO PELL, 40s, banker, in a bowler, nurses a whiskey. Beside him, his young wife, EUGENIA PELL, 20s, in a flapper dress, stares at the floor.

In the corner, BIG DOMINIC, 30s, thick neck, in a dark coat, sits alone and bites a toothpick.

MADAME DUBOIS
(to the whole room)
Another round for the mourners. On the house.

A weak cheer. Horatio raises his glass. Eugenia does not.

HORATIO
(low, to Eugenia)
Smile.

EUGENIA
I am.

HORATIO
Don't look at the door.

EUGENIA
I wasn't.

Shorty slides two drinks across. Dominic watches Horatio. His thumb rubs against his knee.

MADAME DUBOIS
(to Shorty, quietly)
Lock the cellar.

SHORTY
Yes, ma'am.

He moves to the stairs. A heavy tread above. Someone knocks, twice, slow, then once, fast.

Everyone stops. The gramophone needle skips.

A new figure descends the stairs. INSPECTOR CORNELIUS BLACKWOOD, 50s, rain on his shoulders, badge in hand, steps into the lamplight. He surveys the room, the coffins, the crepe.

INSPECTOR BLACKWOOD
Evening, all. I'm told someone has died.

MADAME DUBOIS
(very evenly)
Several, Inspector. Mostly of thirst.

Dominic reaches inside his coat. Horatio's hand finds Eugenia's wrist.

INSPECTOR BLACKWOOD
(to himself)
Always the same room.

He sets his badge on the bar. Takes off his hat. Places it beside it. Nobody breathes.

INSPECTOR BLACKWOOD (CONT'D)
(to the room)
Gentlemen. Ladies. I'm here to pay my respects.

Silence. Then Madame Dubois, slowly, pours him a glass.
